Subject: RenderEngine: let go of finished GPU work once the thread is idle

SurfaceFlinger kept the buffers of a closed app after the app was gone. A DSA build measured
22.7 MiB still held after the Clock app closed: its four window buffers, a buffer of its size
that SurfaceFlinger made for the task snapshot taken as it closed, and one the agent held.

A texture RenderEngine imports for a buffer (AutoBackendTexture) is deleted only when the last
SkImage or SkSurface made from it goes, and Skia lets those go only once it knows the GPU work
that used them has finished, which it learns when it submits again. Until then the texture
keeps the buffer imported into SurfaceFlinger's GPU context and keeps a reference on the
AHardwareBuffer, so the buffer outlives the layer, the client cache entry and the app. DSA's
hardware composer composes every layer on the host, so RenderEngine draws only for screenshots,
virtual displays and the odd client composition, and the next submit may be minutes away.

The RenderEngine thread now waits a second after each stretch of work, and if nothing came
meanwhile it calls RenderEngine::releaseIdleGpuResources. SkiaRenderEngine checks which GPU work
has finished and submits once more, synchronously (SkiaGpuContext::releaseFinishedWork), which
lets Skia drop what finished frames held and makes an OpenGL driver free what it deferred. The
textures no cache holds any more are deleted then. When that let go of any, it logs one line:

  RenderEngine idle: finished GPU work let go of N textures; M remain, K of them for buffers
  SurfaceFlinger still holds

The idle thread does nothing more after that until new work comes, so an idle desktop does not
wake it. A thread that is busy (a frame each vsync) never waits the full second and is not
slowed. Nothing changes after a GPU device loss: the textures go with the lost context.

Applies to frameworks/native at fcbde2bcff56ca1d7bec9cabf8bdca5e288bf103 (Android 16), after 0007.
